v0.0.3 - 2026-11-20
            - Fixed
              * A maze file whose header claims an absurd size is refused with
                an error. The parser allocated the grid the header asked for
                before reading any art, so two short lines piped into `check`
                crashed the tool: `18446744073709551615 1` panicked (exit 101)
                and `100000000 100000000` aborted the process (exit 134).
                Nothing is allocated now until the input is long enough to
                hold the maze it describes.
              * Eller's algorithm no longer carves the last column into one
                straight corridor down to the exit. It forced the last cell of
                every set to open downwards, and the cell in the last column
                is always the last of its set, so every Eller maze had that
                shortcut and some mazes could never be drawn at all, such as
                one of the four 2x2 mazes. A set that opens nothing by chance
                now opens one of its cells chosen at random. Eller mazes for a
                given seed differ from 0.0.2.
              * Eller's algorithm takes time in proportion to the maze. Every
                join relabelled the whole row and every row rebuilt a table
                sized by every set ever made, so 10x80000 took 1.7 s,
                5000x5000 took 16 s and 4.6 GB, a legal 10x2500000 maze most
                of an hour and a 25000000x1 one about two days. 5000x5000
                takes 0.4 s now.
              * Aldous-Broder and Wilson refuse a maze longer than 20000 cells
                on a side. Their random walks take about the square of the
                longer side, so a legal 10x2500000 maze would have run for
                about 44 hours. A single row or column, whose only perfect
                maze is the straight corridor, is carved at once whatever its
                length, and `--algorithm random` only picks an algorithm that
                can build the shape.
              * A reader that stops early no longer makes the tool panic:
                `maze --command generate --width 300 --height 300 | head -1`
                printed "failed printing to stdout: Broken pipe" and exited
                101. A closed pipe now ends
                the output quietly and the command exits on its own verdict,
                and a real write error, such as a full disk, is reported.
              * Bad usage exits with status 1, as the README promises. clap's
                own errors, such as `--width abc`, an unknown flag or a bad
                `--format`, exited 2.
              * Options without `--command` are an error. `maze --input
                m.maze --strict` printed the help and exited 0, so a check
                whose `--command` had been left out passed every maze. A bare
                `maze` still prints the help.
              * An option the command does not read is refused rather than
                ignored. `generate --input saved.maze --output saved.svg`
                exported a brand-new random maze, and `check --output
                report.txt` wrote nothing.
              * `--format` without `--output` writes that format to stdout, as
                `--output -` does. It was ignored, so `generate --format svg >
                maze.svg` saved ASCII art.
              * `solve` refuses an `--output` that is its own `--input`, by
                any name or link. Solving in place replaced the maze with a
                drawing the tool cannot read back, and reported success.
              * `solve` on an unsolvable maze writes nothing to stdout when
                `--output` names where the solution should go. Through
                `--output -` it printed ASCII art where SVG was expected.
              * `solve --output` reports the step count on stderr, which it
                used to drop, and the marked-up maze it writes as text ends
                with a newline, like every other document the tool writes.
              * `--json` with `--output -` is refused before any work is done,
                rather than after building the maze, and `solve` refuses it
                for an unsolvable maze too instead of printing a report.
              * `--input` reads at most 300 MB, more than the largest maze the
                tool makes: `--input /dev/zero` grew to 17 GB in two seconds.
              * `--algorithm random` picks from a stream of its own. The pick
                was the same number the algorithm then drew first, so every
                backtracking maze it chose started by carving east from the
                entrance and every Eller maze it chose opened its first wall.
                A seed may pick a different algorithm than it did in 0.0.2.
              * Braiding draws from a stream of its own, as its comment always
                said; the seed it derived was the algorithm's own stream one
                draw on, so it repeated the algorithm's choices. It also
                leaves alone a dead end an earlier opening has already joined,
                where it used to open a second, needless loop: about a quarter
                of all braid openings. Braided mazes for a given seed differ
                from 0.0.2.
              * Seeds picked at random are below 2^53, so a seed read back from
                `--json` by a reader that stores numbers as doubles, as
                JavaScript does, replays the same maze. A full 64-bit seed was
                rounded on the way almost every time.
              * `Map::carve` from a cell outside the maze returns false instead
                of opening two walls of the outer border: `carve(width, y,
                West)` wrote through an index that aliased the next row.
              * The SVG export no longer clips the outer wall to half the
                thickness of the others, and walls meet at closed corners: the
                drawing is padded by half a wall and the walls have square
                caps. A route of a single cell is drawn as a dot instead of
                vanishing.
              * `check --strict` says so when a maze is imperfect because part
                of it is walled off, instead of blaming loops, and `check`
                reports `Unreachable cells` when there are any.
              * A failed write names the file it was for, and a parse error
                gives the line and column an editor shows.
              * A maze file loads with a UTF-8 byte order mark, and after its
                trailing spaces have been stripped, which editors and
                whitespace hooks do to every maze with an exit.
              * The `--strict` help no longer says an opened maze fails it: an
                entrance and an exit are holes in the border, not loops.
              * The release builds the macOS binaries again. GitHub retired the
                `macos-14` runners, so both macOS jobs failed and 0.0.2
                shipped without them, while the README's install example
                downloaded one.
              * The release page is created once, before the binaries upload,
                with the CHANGELOG section of the tag being released rendered
                as lists. The binary jobs used to race to create it, and the
                notes were the top section run together into one paragraph.
              * The Linux binaries are static musl builds that run on any
                distribution: the 0.0.2 ones needed glibc 2.34 and did not
                start on Debian 11, Ubuntu 20.04 or RHEL 8. The Windows binary
                no longer needs the Visual C++ runtime installed.
              * Release archives store the binary owned by root rather than by
                the build runner's uid 1001, which a root install handed to
                whichever local account had that uid, and carry the licence.
            - Features
              * `solve --json` reports the `format` and `output` it wrote, and
                `check --json` reports `unreachable_cells`.
              * `Map::write_text`, `write_ascii`, `write_ascii_with_path` and
                `write_svg` stream a maze to any writer. Saving and printing
                use them, so they hold a line of the maze at a time instead of
                the whole document, twice over for a saved file.
              * `maze::check_size` tells whether an algorithm can build a size
                without building anything.
              * `AlgorithmType` has `ALL`, `as_str`, `Display` and `FromStr`
                and derives the common traits. `Generator`, `Rng` and the
                algorithms implement `Debug`, and `Generator` is `Send` and
                `Sync`, so it can be handed to another thread.
            - Performance
              * The walls of an SVG are one path of straight runs instead of a
                `<line>` per wall: a 2000x2000 maze is 34 MB instead of 324 MB
                and is written in a third of the time.
              * At 5000x5000 Kruskal's algorithm needs 336 MB instead of 1.5
                GB, Wilson's 74 MB instead of 930 MB, backtracking 68 MB
                instead of 403 MB and Prim's 54 MB instead of 342 MB, from
                compact tables and from writing the maze out a line at a time.
                They draw exactly the mazes they drew before.
              * The random walks of Aldous-Broder and Wilson choose each step
                from a table instead of branching on a random direction:
                5000x5000 takes 13 s instead of 22 s for Aldous-Broder and
                1.1 s instead of 2.1 s for Wilson, with the same mazes.
              * Drawing a solution looks cells up in a bitmap instead of a hash
                set, and the solver keeps one byte per cell instead of eight:
                solving a 5000x5000 maze takes half the time and 241 MB
                instead of 619 MB.
              * `check` walks a perfect maze once instead of twice.
            - Quality
              * `tests/reproducibility.rs` pins 210 seeded mazes, so a
                change that would quietly redraw the mazes people kept by seed
                fails a test. The plain mazes of the five algorithms other than
                Eller are byte for byte what 0.0.2 drew.
              * Tests for each fix above. The parser fuzzing now reaches the
                maze lines (its strings never held a newline), the integration
                and command line tests work in directories of their own that
                they remove again, so two test runs no longer break each
                other, and a vacuous assertion on the solve output checks the
                maze now.
              * Every public item is documented, and `missing_docs` enforces it
                through the clippy gate. `Generator`'s fields are private, so a
                size it has checked cannot be changed afterwards.
              * CI uses the Node 24 versions of its actions, builds and tests
                with `--locked`, builds the image on pull requests, runs once
                per commit instead of twice, checks the crate version against
                the tag, and fails when the toolchain pins disagree. Only a
                release moves the image's `latest` tag.
              * The image builds and runs on the same Debian release, its user
                is no longer created with a warning in a system group, it
                carries the licence, and a local `docker build` no longer sends
                `app/target` along.
v0.0.2 - 2026-11-19
            - Fixed
              * A `v*` tag now publishes the versioned Docker image. On a tag
                build the short-sha tag expanded to `nettrash/maze:-7ef7dc4`,
                an invalid reference that made buildx refuse the whole
                invocation, so `nettrash/maze:0.0.1` never existed and only
                `latest` and the `master` tags did. The sha is prefixed by the
                branch on a branch build and by the tag on a tag build, so
                every tag the action generates is pushable.
              * `aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu` links again in the release job. The
                cross compiler was installed but never named, so the host `cc`
                drove `rust-lld` in x86_64 emulation and the linker refused the
                AArch64-only `--fix-cortex-a53-843419` flag rustc passes
                itself. `app/.cargo/config.toml` points that target at
                `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc`, which a local cross build picks up
                too.
            - Quality
              * The Docker push step no longer runs with `continue-on-error`,
                so an image that never reaches the registry fails the run
                instead of reporting the release green, which is how the
                missing 0.0.1 image went unnoticed.
              * A release the tag creates now carries the newest CHANGELOG
                section as its notes, so the release page is not left empty.
v0.0.1 - 2026-11-19
            - First release.
            - Features
              * Maze Generation with Backtracking Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Prim's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Kruskal's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Aldous-Broder Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Wilson's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Eller's Algorithm.
              * In-memory maze structure with a lossless, versioned text file
                format, so a saved maze loads back exactly as it was written.
              * A library and a command line binary split apart, so the maze
                can be used as a crate as well as from the shell.
              * `generate`, `check` and `solve` commands.
              * Solver that draws the route from the top-left corner to the
                bottom-right corner onto the maze.
              * Reproducible generation through `--seed`, with the seed used
                always reported.
              * SVG export for printing and sharing.
              * Braided mazes (`--braid`) and entrance/exit openings
                (`--entrance-exit`).
              * `list` command, printing the algorithm names the tool accepts so
                a script can enumerate them instead of scraping the help text.
              * `--algorithm random`, which lets the tool choose and reports the
                pick; the choice comes from the seed, so the run stays
                reproducible.
              * `--json` prints one machine-readable object for `check`, `solve`,
                `list` and `generate`, which is how a script reads the seed, the
                step count and the verdicts without parsing prose.
              * `--quiet` drops the banner, the progress line and the "saved to"
                notes; `--json` implies it.
              * `--input -` reads a maze from stdin and `--output -` writes one
                to stdout, so `maze --command generate --output - | maze
                --command solve --input -` works.
              * `check --strict`, which additionally fails when the maze is not
                perfect. Without it a braided maze passes, because a braided
                maze is valid; with it, loops are the failure the caller asked
                to hear about.
              * Prebuilt binaries published with the release for Linux and macOS
                on `x86_64` and `arm64`, and for Windows on `x86_64`, so the tool
                is usable without a Rust toolchain or Docker.
            - Quality
              * Unit, integration and property-based tests; the property tests
                check every algorithm at random sizes including the degenerate
                1x1, 1xN and Nx1 shapes.
              * Command line tests in `app/tests/cli.rs` drive the built binary
                and pin the exit status of every command, which stream each
                message belongs to, the `--json` objects, the pipes and the
                messages for bad usage. They need no dependency beyond the
                standard library.
              * Criterion benchmarks for generation, the variants, the map hot
                paths and file I/O.
              * Continuous integration runs format, clippy, build and test.
              * Docker image published for `nettrash/maze`, built for
                `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64` and tagged with the version, the
                `major.minor` pair and `latest`.
              * A version tag also publishes the prebuilt binaries, built
                `--locked` from the committed `Cargo.lock`, and the smoke-tested
                native targets are run once before upload.
              * Clippy runs with `-D warnings`, so a new lint fails the build
                instead of passing quietly until the next toolchain bump.
              * Documentation builds with `-D warnings` in CI, so a broken
                intra-doc link or a doc example that stopped compiling fails the
                build, and the doc examples run as tests.
              * The Rust toolchain is pinned to 1.100.0 in `rust-toolchain.toml`,
                and the same version is repeated as `rust-version` in
                `Cargo.toml` and as the base image in the Dockerfile, so a local
                build, CI and the image all use one compiler. The file also asks
                for the `rust-analyzer` component, so the editor runs the pinned
                compiler's own analyzer rather than a mismatched bundled one, and
                `Cargo.toml` carries the metadata a publishable crate needs.
              * The library is synchronous through and through: `Generator`
                builds its maze in a plain method rather than an `async fn` that
                never awaited, so neither the crate nor the binary needs a
                runtime. Dropping tokio and chrono leaves `clap` as the only
                dependency and the runtime tree goes from 51 crates to 24.
              * The Dockerfile installs no more `pkg-config`, `libssl-dev`,
                `libpq-dev`, `libpq5` or `libssl3`, none of which anything in the
                dependency tree needs; builds with `--locked` so an image can
                only ever use the reviewed `Cargo.lock`; and the service account
                gets `nologin` rather than a bash that a slim image has never
                had.
            - Performance
              * The grid is stored as one flat row-major allocation instead of
                a vector of rows, so reading a cell is a single index.
              * Walking a cell's neighbours no longer allocates: it fills a
                fixed-size array, which is what every generation algorithm
                does at every step.
              * Verifying and solving a maze index a flat table by cell rather
                than hashing coordinates, which is about nine times faster.
              * The `check` command counts dead ends directly instead of
                building the list to take its length.
            - Fixed
              * `--width 0` and `--height 0` are rejected instead of printing
                an empty maze and exiting 0, so a script can tell that no maze
                was produced.
              * A maze bigger than 25 million cells is refused before anything
                is allocated, with the cell count and the limit in the message.
                `--width 100000 --height 100000` used to run until memory ran
                out; it now fails immediately.
              * `check` exits with a failure status when the maze cannot be
                solved, having reported `Solvable: false` and still exited 0.
                Scripts can gate on it now, and it agrees with `solve`, which
                always failed on the same maze.
              * The banner, progress and summary lines go to stderr, so stdout
                carries only the maze and the `check` verdicts and can be
                redirected to a file.
              * The library no longer prints. The progress line moved to the
                command line tool, which leaves `Generator` silent for anyone
                using it as a crate.
              * The copyright year in the banner is a constant kept in step with
                `LICENSE`, rather than a clock reading that printed a backwards
                range on a machine set before the founding year.
